# Reflective agent

A **reflective agent** is a system that holds a representation of its own
models, rules or behaviour and uses the results of its activity to revise
them. The term is used in this vault for two neighbouring classes of the
discussion's taxonomy, reflective adaptive systems and self-modeling
cognition. It is a working term of the discussion and not an established
technical one.

## Description

### Two classes, one card

The [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] ends with two classes that concern agents.

| Class | Mechanism | Examples |
|---|---|---|
| Reflective adaptive systems | Models and rules are revised using results | Scientists, reflective agents |
| Self-modeling cognition | The reasoner represents itself in its own model | Human self-models, hypothetical AI architectures |

The first is about what gets changed, the second about what gets represented.
An agent in the first class may revise its rules without any explicit picture
of itself. An agent in the second has such a picture, whether or not it acts
on it. The interesting case is both at once: the self-model is what the
revision works from.

### Evolving reflection applied to agents

The discussion separates static reflection, where a system can express some
of its own properties, from evolving reflection, where those reflective
results change the rules. A formal theory that encodes its own proofs is the
first. A [[reflection-progression]] is the second, and a reflective agent is
whatever drives it.

Two depths of revision are distinguished.

1. **Revising the model.** The agent changes what it believes or how it acts:
   the next theory, the next version of a program.
2. **Revising the criteria of revision.** The agent changes how it decides
   what to change. In the notation of the discussion a stage is a pair
   $(T_n, R_n)$, a theory together with the rule for extending it, and both
   move.

The second depth is what the sixth of the [[definitions-of-strange-loop]]
describes: a system that uses a self-model to revise future models, rules, or
criteria for revision.

The same shape appears in the cycle that links compression to prediction.
That cycle has an outer loop of observing, modelling, predicting and
evaluating, and a second loop in which the system builds "a model of the
modeling process" and feeds it back into how regularities are found. The
second loop is the reflective one.

### The agent of the essay

The essay on AI-oriented languages describes the same thing without the
vocabulary. It asks for an environment where an agent can reason about a
program, execute it, inspect the result, and revise its own description of
the problem. Specification, program, observations and agent instructions are
all inspectable artifacts, so the agent's instructions are among the things
it can read and propose to change. The essay is careful that this does not
automatically make a [[strange-loop]], and that it overcomes no
incompleteness result.

### What is not claimed

- A reflective agent need not be human. A program can carry out a progression
  of theories. See [[human-in-the-loop]].
- A reflective agent is not thereby incomplete in Gödel's sense. The claim
  that every sufficiently reflective intelligence meets an analogous limit is
  a conjecture, the seventh definition, and not a theorem.
- An agent that merely repeats a cycle of act and observe is a
  [[feedback-loop]]. Reflection starts when the cycle passes through a
  representation of the agent.

## Relations

- It covers two classes of the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: instance-of}, adaptive systems and self-modeling cognition.
- It is what drives a [[reflection-progression]] {relation: uses} when no human does.
- It can take the seat of the [[human-in-the-loop]] {relation: contrasts}.
- Its defining operations are [[reflection]] {relation: uses} on its own process and [[revision]] {relation: uses} of its rules.
- It is more than a [[feedback-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: the cycle passes through a self-model.
- It is the sixth of the [[definitions-of-strange-loop]] {relation: example-of}, the evolving reflective one.
- The second loop of [[compression-and-prediction]] {relation: example-of} is a reflective agent in miniature.
- Its operations should be offered as a [[typed-transformation]] {relation: uses} so that revisions carry evidence.
- It bears on the question [[ai-support-in-hql]] {relation: answers}: this is the kind of agent a card would host.
- It is a kind of [[agent]] {relation: is-a}: one that uses a model of itself to revise itself.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[reflection-progression]].** Theories extended one undecided statement
  at a time, possibly with changing criteria. It is the formal skeleton of
  what a reflective agent does.
- **[[human-in-the-loop]].** A person at the step where results are
  interpreted. The discussion's correction is that the step matters and the
  person is optional.
- **[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]].** Eight classes from recursive
  generation to self-modeling cognition. Reflective agents are its last two,
  the only ones that change over time.
- **[[reflection]].** The operation that takes a process and its outcomes and
  returns an analysis of them. An agent is reflective when the process
  analysed is its own.

